      "index": 0,
      "blockIndex": 0,
      "paragraphInBlock": 0,
      "text": "Place a plano-convex lens on a flat glass plate with its curved face downward, using a lens whose curved surface has a large radius of curvature.\nA very thin air layer remains between the lens and the plate, and its thickness changes with distance from their contact point.\nUnder suitable illumination, two reflected beams interfere and form concentric rings called **Newton's rings**."
    },
    {
      "index": 1,
      "blockIndex": 1,
      "paragraphInBlock": 0,
      "text": "Use a lens with a gently curved surface and carefully examine the place where it touches the glass plate, preferably with a magnifying glass.\nUnder the reflected-light conditions discussed in the source, an ideal contact gives a dark spot near the centre, with small coloured rings around it under white light."
    },
    {
      "index": 2,
      "blockIndex": 1,
      "paragraphInBlock": 1,
      "text": "The spacing between neighbouring rings of the same kind decreases outward, as shown in Color Plate III (彩图 III，1).\nNewton (牛顿) carefully studied rings formed with white light and also rings formed with monochromatic illumination."
    },
    {
      "index": 3,
      "blockIndex": 1,
      "paragraphInBlock": 2,
      "text": "For the same apparatus, ring order, and kind of bright or dark ring, the radius increases when illumination changes from violet to red, with red giving the largest radius, as shown in Color Plate III (彩图 III，2及3).\nThese effects can be compared experimentally, but rings of different orders or different kinds must not be mixed in one comparison."
    },
    {
      "index": 4,
      "blockIndex": 2,
      "paragraphInBlock": 0,
      "text": "The source explains that Newton (牛顿) did not give the wave explanation used here, whereas Young (杨) explained the rings by treating light as a wave.\nFollowing Young's reasoning, consider light of a definite wavelength incident approximately normally on a plano-convex lens, as illustrated by the light path in Figure 199."
    },
    {
      "index": 5,
      "blockIndex": 2,
      "paragraphInBlock": 1,
      "text": "Wave $1$ is reflected at the glass-air boundary on the lens's curved surface, while wave $2$ enters the air layer and is reflected at the lower air-glass boundary.\nThe reflected beams come from one incident beam and can have the same wavelength and a stable relative phase, which is the coherence needed here."
    },
    {
      "index": 6,
      "blockIndex": 2,
      "paragraphInBlock": 2,
      "text": "Phase tells us which stage of a vibration cycle a wave has reached, and a stable phase relationship makes a stable bright-and-dark pattern possible.\nThe additional distance travelled by wave $2$ compared with wave $1$ changes this relationship, but the phase change caused by reflection must also be included rather than comparing geometrical distances alone."
    },
    {
      "index": 7,
      "blockIndex": 5,
      "paragraphInBlock": 0,
      "text": "When propagation and reflection are counted together, a total delay of the second wave relative to the first equivalent to an integer number of whole wavelengths① puts the waves at the same stage of vibration when they meet.\nTheir superposition then strengthens the resultant vibration and gives a brighter region.\nThis whole-wavelength condition concerns the total delay including reflection, not merely the additional geometrical distance through the air layer."
    },
    {
      "index": 8,
      "blockIndex": 6,
      "paragraphInBlock": 0,
      "text": "① The source footnote points out that the effective path difference of wave $1$ and wave $2$ is not simply twice the air-layer thickness at their point of incidence.\nReflection at the lower glass plate changes the wave's phase by $\\pi$, which is equivalent, for this comparison, to adding a path difference of $\\lambda/2$."
    },
    {
      "index": 9,
      "blockIndex": 6,
      "paragraphInBlock": 1,
      "text": "At an ideal contact point, the air-layer thickness is zero, so there is no propagation path difference in the air layer, but the half-cycle reflection phase difference remains.\nThe reflected beams therefore weaken each other there, producing the dark centre."
    },
    {
      "index": 10,
      "blockIndex": 6,
      "paragraphInBlock": 2,
      "text": "If their vibration strengths are equal, cancellation can be complete in the ideal case.\nThe source does not develop this complication further, and\nthe important point here is why this contribution must enter the total phase delay, not that light physically travels an extra distance."
    },
    {
      "index": 11,
      "blockIndex": 7,
      "paragraphInBlock": 0,
      "text": "Conversely, a total delay including reflection equivalent to an odd number of half-wavelengths puts the two waves at opposite stages of vibration when they meet.\nTheir superposition then reduces the resultant vibration by cancellation and gives a darker region.\nEqual vibration strengths allow complete cancellation, while unequal strengths generally allow only partial cancellation."
    }
  ],
